Use a **decorator factory**: a function that accepts your dictionary and returns a class decorator, which loops over the entries and attaches each function with `setattr`. Since functions assigned to a class become bound methods (descriptor protocol), the lambdas taking `self` work as-is.

```python
methods = {
    "walk": lambda self: print(f"{self.name} is walking"),
    "run":  lambda self: print(f"{self.name} is running"),
}

def add_methods(methods):
    def decorator(cls):
        for name, func in methods.items():
            setattr(cls, name, func)
        return cls
    return decorator

@add_methods(methods)
class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

dog = Animal("Rex")
dog.walk()  # Rex is walking
dog.run()   # Rex is running
```

Your earlier attempt only added one method because a plain decorator wraps a single object. Putting the loop inside a class decorator adds any number of methods in one line (`@add_methods(methods)`).

If decorator syntax isn't required, the equivalent without it is a one-liner after the class definition:

```python
for name, func in methods.items():
    setattr(Animal, name, func)
```

Both approaches mutate the class itself, so all instances get the new methods.
